From a9201f604120401e47c8f99ec69f37b335126d21 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gavin John <gavinnjohn@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 04:09:56 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 67/77] aout: clear the latency callback before the module is
 asked for

The callbacks an output module may leave alone are set to NULL before
module_need(), because a module only assigns the ones it implements and the
core has no other way to know which those are. latency_get was added to the
struct without being added to that list, so it was whatever the allocation
happened to hold for any module that does not set it - every module written
before it existed, in the tree or out of it.

Nothing was seen to go wrong: the object is allocated zeroed, so the value
is NULL in practice and aout_OutputLatencyGet() rejects it as it should.
That is luck rather than design, and the neighbouring assignments already
say what the design is.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/audio_output/output.c | 1 +
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)

diff --git a/src/audio_output/output.c b/src/audio_output/output.c
index e6832cb2a7..a1b340efd2 100644
--- a/src/audio_output/output.c
+++ b/src/audio_output/output.c
@@ -238,6 +238,7 @@ audio_output_t *aout_New (vlc_object_t *parent)
     /* Audio output module initialization */
     aout->start = NULL;
     aout->stop = NULL;
+    aout->latency_get = NULL;
     aout->volume_set = NULL;
     aout->mute_set = NULL;
     aout->device_select = NULL;
